<Legal
  title="Accessibility"
  description="What we have built to make this site usable for everyone, what we know is imperfect, and how to tell us when something does not work."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
  current="accessibility"
>
  <p>
    This site deals with subjects people often reach for at a difficult moment.
    It should work for everybody who needs it, including anyone using a screen
    reader, a keyboard, magnification, or a slow connection.
  </p>

  <p>
    We aim to meet <strong>WCAG 2.2 Level AA</strong>. We are not claiming a
    formal audit — we are telling you what we have done and what we know is
    still imperfect.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in place</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Every page has one main heading and a logical heading order.</li>
    <li>Every page can be operated by keyboard alone, with a visible focus outline.</li>
    <li>A "skip to content" link is the first thing a keyboard reaches.</li>
    <li>The help drawer traps focus while open, closes on <strong>Escape</strong>, and returns focus where it was.</li>
    <li>Images that carry meaning have alternative text. Decorative images are marked empty so screen readers skip them.</li>
    <li>Contrast is measured, not assumed: body text is 13:1 against its background and the lightest secondary text is 4.5:1, both at or above the AA threshold.</li>
    <li>Animation is limited, and all of it stops for anyone whose system requests reduced motion.</li>
    <li>The layout reflows to a single column and stays readable at 320 pixels wide and at 200% zoom.</li>
    <li>Pages are plain HTML that render without JavaScript. Nothing essential to reading is behind a script.</li>
    <li>Times are given in words with time-zone conversions, not only as a graphic.</li>
    <li>The site is light on data — no tracking, no heavy scripts, small images — so it loads on a poor connection.</li>
    <li>Typefaces are served from this site, so text renders even when outside font services are blocked or unreachable.</li>
    <li>Episode pages show a still image rather than loading a video player, which is far lighter and works on a metered connection.</li>
    <li><a href="/search">Search</a> runs on your own device and needs no network round-trip once the page has loaded.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    An automated structural check runs on every change and blocks publication if
    a page loses its main heading, an image loses its alternative text, or a
    link breaks. That is not a substitute for testing with real assistive
    technology, but it stops the common regressions.
  </p>

  <h2>What we know is imperfect</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Embedded YouTube players</strong> are built by YouTube. Their
      keyboard behaviour and captions are outside our control. Captions depend
      on what has been added to each video, and older episodes may only have
      automatic ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Episode transcripts do not exist yet.</strong> This is the largest
      gap on the site. Audio and video are unusable for anyone deaf or hard of
      hearing without them, and transcripts are planned.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Some artwork carries text inside the image</strong>, which a
      screen reader cannot read. Where that art announces a show time, the same
      information is written in text nearby.
    </li>
    <li>
      We have not yet tested with every screen reader and browser combination.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The quick-exit control</h2>

  <p>
    The <a href="/harbor-of-hope">Harbor of Hope</a> page has a button that
    moves you away from the page quickly, and it also responds to pressing{" "}
    <strong>Escape</strong> three times. Both work by keyboard. Please read the
    safety notes on that page: it does not clear your browsing history, and it
    cannot defeat monitoring software on your device.
  </p>

  <h2>Tell us when something does not work</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      If any part of this site is difficult or impossible for you to use, please
      use <a href="/contact">the contact page</a>. Tell us the page
      and what happened, and what you were using if you know it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a formality. Accessibility problems are treated as defects
      rather than requests, and if something on this site is stopping somebody
      from reaching help, it gets fixed first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you need something on this site in another format — a transcript, plain
    text, or large print — ask and we will get it to you.
  </p>
</Legal>
